---
// /admin/setup — CAN SOMEBODY WHO HAS NEVER SEEN THIS PRODUCT OPERATE IT ON DAY ONE?
//
// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. Every other pass on this console fixed faults. This one answers a different
// question: a person signs in for the first time, against a database with almost nothing in it, and
// has to work out what to do. Until now the answer was nowhere. There was no first-run surface of
// any kind; grep across src/pages/admin and src/lib for "setup", "first-run" or "getting started"
// returned only unrelated prose. The dashboard offered "New Role", "Add Employee", "Leave Requests"
// and "Run Payroll" as unordered peers, and nothing anywhere said that departments come before
// employees, that employees come before the organization graph, or that a payroll run cannot be
// undone. Eleven admin pages, and no statement of which one to open first.
//
// TWO THINGS ARE ON THIS PAGE AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT PROBLEMS.
//
//   1. THE ORDER. Six steps, in the order they have to be done, each with a live count, each saying
//      what stays broken until it is done. The count comes from src/lib/day-one.ts, which reads one
//      COUNT per step behind safeRows so a single missing table cannot blank the whole checklist —
//      and a missing table is reported as "not set up yet", which is what it means, rather than as
//      an outage.
//
//   2. WHAT EXISTS AT ALL. The sidebar in src/layouts/AdminLayout.astro is a hand-maintained list
//      that has drifted from src/lib/admin-nav.ts, the module written to replace it: forty-nine
//      hrefs in admin-nav.ts are absent from the rendered sidebar, and three live pages
//      (/admin/org/structure, /admin/hr/payroll/components, /admin/hr/payroll/claims) are in
//      NEITHER list. A first-time operator cannot find roughly a third of the product and nothing
//      on any screen tells them it is there. This pass may not edit src/layouts/, so the fix here is
//      a reachable index: the directory below is built from admin-nav.ts through buildAdminNav(),
//      which filters per person and fails closed, plus the three screens that are in no list at all.
//      It does not widen anybody's access — the middleware and each page's own gate still decide —
//      it only stops the product hiding from the people who own it.
//
// NO DATABASE WRITE HAPPENS ON THIS PAGE, and no DDL. It counts and it links. Where the next action
// is a migration this console does not perform, it prints the command for a human to run rather
// than offering a button that pretends to.
import AdminLayout from '@/layouts/AdminLayout.astro';
import DayOne from '@/components/admin/DayOne.astro';
import { readSetupStatus, outstandingSteps, uncheckableSteps, type SetupStatus } from '@/lib/day-one';
import { buildAdminNav, isNavGroup, type AdminNavEntry, type AdminNavItem } from '@/lib/admin-nav';
import { canAccessSection } from '@/lib/auth/permissions';
import { clip } from '@/lib/page-safety';

const user = Astro.locals.user;
if (!user) return Astro.redirect('/admin/login');
if (user.role === 'applicant') return Astro.redirect('/portal');

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONSTANTS FIRST. `const` is not hoisted; a binding declared under its first reader has taken pages
// down on this project.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CREATE THE MODULE TABLES, from a button rather than a console command.
//
// This platform has no migration runner: each module creates its own tables the first time somebody
// opens it, so /api/health reports "degraded — N module table(s) not yet created" until a human
// happens to visit every surface. The API endpoint that does them all deliberately needs an admin
// session, which meant pasting fetch() into DevTools — a step that failed twice on a laptop whose
// F12 is bound to a vendor utility. A setup action a person cannot reach is not a setup action.
//
// Declared here, above the handler, because const is not hoisted.
const BOOTSTRAP_MODULES: Array<{ name: string; run: () => Promise<unknown> }> = [
  { name: 'auth-registry', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/auth/registry')).ensureRegistrySchema() },
  { name: 'rbac', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/rbac/store')).ensureRbacSchema() },
  { name: 'observability-health', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/observability-health')).ensureObservabilitySchema() },
  { name: 'feature-flags', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/observability')).ensureFlagSchema() },
  { name: 'knowledge-sync', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/knowledge-sync')).ensureSyncSchema() },
  { name: 'mail', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/mail')).ensureMailSchema() },
  // Discovery. Both its tables are read by /aquintutor/search and /admin/search, and created by
  // nothing else on this deployment -- see db/search-index-schema.sql.
  { name: 'search-index', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/search-index')).ensureSearchSchema() },
  // HIRING DECISION SUPPORT AND THE CAPABILITY SPINE IT READS, ADDED 2026-08-24.
  //
  // WHY: /admin/applications/[id]/decision refused to record a real decision in production with
  // relation "hiring_decisions" does not exist. That table's only creator is
  // ensureHiringDecisionSchema(), and src/lib/hiring-decision.ts shipped on 2026-08-23, the same day
  // production stopped running request-path DDL — so it has never been created on the live database
  // and this button, the one control an operator has for "create what is missing", could not create
  // it either.
  //
  // ORDER MATTERS HERE, WHICH IS UNUSUAL FOR THIS LIST. hr_role_requirements and hr_skill_relations
  // both REFERENCE hr_skills, and ensureSpineSchema() runs its CREATEs in one sequence that stops at
  // the first failure — so the skill catalogue is created first, then the spine, then the decision
  // table. Run the other way round, the spine's later statements fail and the failure is silent.
  { name: 'capability-catalogue', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/performance-schema')).ensurePerformanceSchema() },
  { name: 'person-spine', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/person-spine')).ensureSpineSchema() },
  { name: 'capability-readings', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/match')).ensureMatchSchema() },
  { name: 'hiring-decision', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/hiring-decision')).ensureHiringDecisionSchema() },
  { name: 'error-log', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/logger')).recentErrors(1) },
  { name: 'job-queue', run: async () => (await import('@/lib/job-queue')).claimBatch(0) },
];

let bootstrapOk = '';
let bootstrapFailed: string[] = [];

if (Astro.request.method === 'POST') {
  const form = await Astro.request.formData().catch(() => null);
  if (form && String(form.get('action') || '') === 'bootstrap') {
    const { can } = await import('@/lib/auth/permissions');
    if (!can(user as any, 'settings.edit')) {
      bootstrapFailed = ['You do not have permission to change platform configuration.'];
    } else {
      // INSIDE allowingDdl(), BECAUSE CREATING THESE TABLES IS THE BUTTON'S ONLY PURPOSE.
      //
      // db.execute refuses DDL when SCHEMA_BOOTSTRAP is off, which is the production default and is
      // right for every request-time bootstrap that fires because somebody opened a page. This is
      // the opposite case: a named operator pressed a button whose entire contract is "create the
      // module tables". Without this the loop below runs, every module reports success, and nothing
      // is created — the exact silent failure the verification step under it already exists to
      // catch, arriving through a new door.
      //
      // Scoped by AsyncLocalStorage to this handler only, and the settings.edit check above is still
      // what decides who gets here.
      const { allowingDdl } = await import('@/lib/schema-bootstrap');
      let done = 0;
      await allowingDdl(async () => {
      for (const m of BOOTSTRAP_MODULES) {
        try { await m.run(); done++; }
        catch (e: any) {
          // The real Postgres reason is on e.cause; e.message is only the failed SQL. Never swallowed.
          const why = e?.cause?.message || e?.message || 'unknown error';
          console.error('[setup/bootstrap] ' + m.name + ' failed:', why);
          bootstrapFailed.push(m.name + ': ' + why);
        }
      }
      });
      // VERIFY, do not count. ensureOnce() ends in a catch that swallows, so every ensure above can
      // return cleanly having created nothing — which is exactly what happened: this reported eight
      // modules ready while /api/health went on finding all ten tables missing. Ask the database.
      const EXPECTED = ['audit_log', 'edu_cron_runs', 'edu_error_log', 'edu_feature_flags',
        'edu_job_log', 'edu_jobs', 'edu_releases', 'edu_search_index', 'edu_search_queries',
        'edu_sync_queue', 'mail_config', 'rbac_audit',
        // The hiring decision stack, added with the three modules above. Without these six names
        // the panel would report every expected table present while the decision screen went on
        // refusing to record a decision - a verify step is only worth having over the full list.
        'hiring_decisions', 'match_evaluations', 'hr_persons', 'hr_person_identities',
        'hr_role_requirements',
        'hr_skills', 'hr_employee_skills'];
      let present: string[] = [];
      let verified = false;
      try {
        const { db } = await import('@/lib/db');
        const { sql } = await import('drizzle-orm');
        // = ANY(${array}) fails with "op ANY/ALL (array) requires array on right side": the driver
        // sends a JS array as a plain parameter, not a typed text[]. An IN list of individual
        // placeholders is unambiguous and needs no cast.
        const names = sql.join(EXPECTED.map((t) => sql`${t}`), sql`, `);
        const r: any = await db.execute(sql`
          SELECT table_name FROM information_schema.tables
          WHERE table_schema = 'public' AND table_name IN (${names})`);
        const rows = Array.isArray(r) ? r : (r?.rows || []);
        present = rows.map((x: any) => String(x.table_name));
        verified = true;
      } catch (e: any) {
        bootstrapFailed.push('could not verify: ' + (e?.cause?.message || e?.message || 'unknown'));
      }
      // A failed check means we do not KNOW. Reporting the tables as missing on the strength of a
      // query that never ran is the same false certainty this whole panel exists to remove — it is
      // what made the first run say eight modules were ready when nothing had been created.
      if (verified) {
        const missing = EXPECTED.filter((t) => !present.includes(t));
        bootstrapOk = present.length + ' of ' + EXPECTED.length + ' tables exist.';
        if (missing.length > 0) bootstrapFailed.push('still missing: ' + missing.join(', '));
      } else {
        bootstrapOk = 'The modules ran. Whether the tables now exist could not be checked.';
      }
    }
  }
}

const CARD = 'background:#0f0f14;border:1px solid #1a1a1f;border-radius:14px;padding:16px 18px;';
const HD = "font-size:10.5px;color:#8a8a94;text-transform:uppercase;font-family:'Geist Mono',monospace;letter-spacing:0.12em;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 10px;";

// THE CHECKLIST. Never throws; a step it could not count says so rather than reading as "not done".
const statuses: SetupStatus[] = await readSetupStatus();
const todo = outstandingSteps(statuses);
const unknown = uncheckableSteps(statuses);
const doneCount = statuses.filter((s) => s.state === 'done').length;

/** The org-graph step, singled out because it is the prerequisite that halts the most modules. */
const graphStatus = statuses.find((s) => s.step.id === 'orgGraph') || null;

// THE DIRECTORY. buildAdminNav() answers for THIS person and fails closed on any error — no session,
// a degraded permission resolve, a thrown query all return Dashboard and Notifications only, never
// the full tree. A menu that opens up when the database blinks is the same mistake as a gate that
// does, so the failure mode here is a short list, not a long one.
let navEntries: AdminNavEntry[] = [];
let navError = '';
try {
  navEntries = await buildAdminNav(user as any, { locals: Astro.locals });
} catch (e: any) {
  navError = String(e?.cause?.message || e?.message || 'unknown error');
  console.error('[admin/setup] admin nav could not be built -', navError);
}

/** Flatten the tree into { group, items } for a printed index rather than a menu. */
const directory: { label: string; items: AdminNavItem[] }[] = [];
const loose: AdminNavItem[] = [];
for (const entry of navEntries) {
  if (isNavGroup(entry)) {
    if (entry.children.length) directory.push({ label: entry.label, items: entry.children });
  } else {
    loose.push(entry as AdminNavItem);
  }
}
if (loose.length) directory.unshift({ label: 'Always available', items: loose });

// THE THREE SCREENS THAT ARE IN NEITHER NAVIGATION LIST. Each is gated on the section its own page
// and the middleware already require, so listing it here reveals nothing a person could not reach by
// typing the URL — and hides it from anybody who could not.
const mayHr = await canAccessSection(user as any, 'hr', 'view');
const mayPayroll = await canAccessSection(user as any, 'payroll', 'view');
const unlisted: { href: string; label: string; note: string; show: boolean }[] = [
  {
    href: '/admin/org/structure',
    label: 'Organization structure',
    note: 'Teams, positions and who sits in them. Referenced by no menu and by no other page — reachable today only by typing this URL. Read the note under "The organization graph" below before using it: it does NOT record reporting relationships.',
    show: mayHr,
  },
  {
    href: '/admin/hr/payroll/components',
    label: 'Salary components',
    note: 'What a payroll run deducts. In neither navigation list, and a run created before it is configured deducts nothing and cannot be regenerated.',
    show: mayPayroll,
  },
  {
    href: '/admin/hr/payroll/claims',
    label: 'Expense claims',
    note: 'Claim review. In neither navigation list; linked only from the payroll tab strip.',
    show: mayPayroll,
  },
];
const unlistedVisible = unlisted.filter((u) => u.show);

/** One line per step for the header summary, without ever printing a count we could not read. */
function stepCountWords(s: SetupStatus): string {
  if (s.state === 'unknown' || s.count === null) return 'could not be counted';
  if (s.count === 0) return 'none yet';
  return s.count + ' ' + s.step.unit + (s.count === 1 ? '' : 's');
}
---
<AdminLayout title="Set up the platform" activeNav="dashboard">
  <div class="setup-wrap">

    <div class="setup-head">
      <p class="setup-eyebrow">First run</p>
      <h1 class="setup-title">Set up the platform</h1>
      <p class="setup-sub">
        Six things, in this order. Each one says what stops working until it is done. The counts
        beside them are read live: a step that could not be counted says so rather than showing you a
        zero it cannot stand behind.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* THE MODULE TABLES. A button, because the alternative was a fetch() pasted into a browser
        console — a step that is not reachable on every keyboard and is not a thing to ask an
        operator to do. Idempotent: pressing it again is harmless. */}
    <form method="POST" class="setup-boot">
      <input type="hidden" name="action" value="bootstrap" />
      <div>
        <p class="setup-boot-h">Module tables</p>
        <p class="setup-boot-p">
          Each module creates its own tables the first time it is opened, so an untouched module has
          none and its first real user is the one who finds that out. This creates them all now.
          Nothing is deleted or changed, and pressing it twice does nothing the second time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <button type="submit">Create module tables</button>
    </form>

    {bootstrapOk !== '' && bootstrapFailed.length === 0 && (
      <div class="setup-alert setup-alert-ok"><p>{bootstrapOk}</p></div>
    )}
    {bootstrapFailed.length > 0 && (
      <div class="setup-alert setup-alert-bad">
        <p>{bootstrapOk === '' ? 'This did not run.' : bootstrapOk + ' The rest did not:'}</p>
        <ul>{bootstrapFailed.map((f) => (<li>{clip(f, 240)}</li>))}</ul>
      </div>
    )}

    {unknown.length > 0 && (
      <div class="setup-alert setup-alert-bad">
        <p>
          {unknown.length === 1 ? 'One step could not be checked' : unknown.length + ' steps could not be checked'},
          so this page does not know whether {unknown.length === 1 ? 'it has' : 'they have'} been done.
          Treat the checklist below as incomplete rather than as an answer.
        </p>
        <ul>
          {unknown.map((s) => (
            <li><strong>{s.step.label}</strong> — {clip(s.error || 'unknown database error', 240)}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    )}

    {unknown.length === 0 && todo.length === 0 && (
      <div class="setup-alert setup-alert-ok">
        <p>
          Every required step has something recorded against it. That is not the same as being
          finished — one department and one employee satisfy this checklist — but nothing below is
          blocking a whole module any more.
        </p>
      </div>
    )}

    {todo.length > 0 && (
      <div class="setup-alert setup-alert-todo">
        <p>
          {doneCount} of {statuses.length} steps have something recorded.
          Start at <a href={todo[0].step.href}>{todo[0].step.label}</a> — it is the earliest one with
          nothing in it, and the steps under it depend on it.
        </p>
      </div>
    )}

    <ol class="setup-list">
      {statuses.map((s) => (
        <li class={'setup-step ' + (s.state === 'done' ? 'is-done' : s.state === 'unknown' ? 'is-unknown' : 'is-todo')}>
          <div class="setup-step-head">
            <span class="setup-num" aria-hidden="true">{s.step.order}</span>
            <div class="setup-step-title">
              <h2>{s.step.label}{s.step.optional ? ' (only if you need it)' : ''}</h2>
              <p class="setup-count">{stepCountWords(s)}</p>
            </div>
            <span class={'setup-badge ' + (s.state === 'done' ? 'b-done' : s.state === 'unknown' ? 'b-unknown' : 'b-todo')}>
              {s.state === 'done' ? 'recorded' : s.state === 'unknown' ? 'unknown' : 'not yet'}
            </span>
          </div>

          <p class="setup-why">{s.step.why}</p>
          <p class="setup-blocks"><strong>Until this exists:</strong> {s.step.blocks}</p>

          {s.state === 'unknown' && s.error && (
            <p class="setup-reason">{clip(s.error, 300)}</p>
          )}

          <p class="setup-do"><a href={s.step.href}>{s.step.action}</a></p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <!-- =========================================================================================
         THE ORGANIZATION GRAPH — the prerequisite that halts a dozen modules, explained on a screen
         that is actually reachable. Until now the only place it was named was a sentence repeated
         verbatim on fifteen admin pages, every one of which is missing from the rendered sidebar.
         ========================================================================================= -->
    <section id="org-graph" class="setup-section">
      <p style={HD}>The organization graph</p>

      {graphStatus && graphStatus.state === 'unknown' ? (
        <DayOne
          state="unreadable"
          thing="The organization graph"
          error={graphStatus.error}
        />
      ) : graphStatus && graphStatus.state === 'todo' ? (
        <DayOne
          state="unconfigured"
          thing="The organization graph"
          unconfiguredBody="It carries no active relationship, so no approval anywhere in this product has anybody to route to."
        />
      ) : (
        <div class="setup-alert setup-alert-ok">
          <p>
            The graph carries {graphStatus && graphStatus.count !== null ? graphStatus.count : 'some'} active
            relationship{graphStatus && graphStatus.count === 1 ? '' : 's'}. Approvals can resolve an
            owner. That does not mean every person has a manager — a screen that says nobody is
            recorded for one employee is telling you about that employee, not about the graph.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}

      <div class="setup-prose">
        <h3>What it is</h3>
        <p>
          Relationships in this product are per-row facts between two employee records — this person
          reports to that person, this person heads that department, this person may approve for that
          scope. They are never read from an account role. That is deliberate and it is why a "manager"
          role name grants nobody any authority over anybody.
        </p>

        <h3>What records a relationship today, and what does not</h3>
        <p>
          One screen in this console writes to the graph: assigning a manager to a project on a
          project's own page records a <em>project manager</em> relationship. Nothing else does.
          <strong> Reporting manager, department head, approval owner and delegate have no screen at
          all</strong> — they are seeded from the employee records by a migration, listed below, that a
          person runs by hand.
        </p>
        <p class="setup-warn">
          <strong>/admin/org/structure is not this.</strong> It records teams, positions and who sits
          in them, in a separate set of tables. Building the whole structure there leaves the graph
          exactly as empty as it was, and every screen that says the organization graph is not set up
          will keep saying it. Two different things are called "the org" and only one of them routes
          an approval.
        </p>

        <h3>How it is seeded</h3>
        <p>
          By running these against the database, in this order, as the person who holds the
          credentials. This console does not run them and there is no button here that claims to:
          they write rows to production, and the established pattern on this project is that a
          migration is handed over rather than fired from a web page.
        </p>
        <pre class="setup-cmd">psql "$DATABASE_URL" -f db/org-graph-schema.sql
psql "$DATABASE_URL" -f db/org-graph-backfill.sql
psql "$DATABASE_URL" -f db/org-graph-validate.sql</pre>
        <p>
          Read the output of the third one. It is the only thing that tells you the second one did
          what it was supposed to: a green message from a script proves the script ran, not that it
          did the intended work against the intended database. If it reports cycles, orphans or
          unmapped managers, fix those and run the backfill and the validation again.
          <code>db/org-graph-rollback.sql</code> undoes it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The backfill reads <code>hr_employees.reporting_manager_id</code>, so it can only seed
          relationships for employees who already have a manager recorded on their employment tab. If
          step 2 above is still empty, running it now will insert nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- =========================================================================================
         BEFORE THE FIRST PAYROLL RUN. Its own block because it is the one action on this console
         that cannot be undone, and the only screen offering it is a screen a curious operator opens
         on their first afternoon.
         ========================================================================================= -->
    <section class="setup-section">
      <p style={HD}>Before the first payroll run</p>
      <div class="setup-prose">
        <p class="setup-warn">
          A payroll run cannot be regenerated, deleted or voided anywhere in this product. Creating
          one for a month permanently consumes that month: a second run for the same period is
          refused. Do not press Run Payroll to see what it does.
        </p>
        <p>Before the first run, in this order:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/admin/hr/employees">Employee records exist</a> and each person who is to be paid is active.</li>
          <li><a href="/admin/hr/payroll?tab=salary">Pay is recorded</a> for each of them. A run refuses to write a payslip for anybody with no base salary and no salary structure, and lists them by name instead of paying them a zero that balances.</li>
          <li><a href="/admin/hr/payroll/components">Salary components are configured</a>. A run applies exactly what is configured and assumes nothing, so a run created before this deducts no provident fund, no state insurance, no professional tax and no withholding — and cannot be redone afterwards.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- =========================================================================================
         THE DIRECTORY.
         ========================================================================================= -->
    <section class="setup-section">
      <p style={HD}>Everything you can open</p>
      <p class="setup-sub" style="margin-top:0;">
        The sidebar does not list every screen you have access to. This is the full set for your
        account, built from the permission registry. A screen you cannot open is not listed here and
        listing one here would not let you open it — the page and the middleware still decide.
      </p>

      {navError && (
        <DayOne
          state="unreadable"
          thing="Your menu"
          error={navError}
        />
      )}

      {unlistedVisible.length > 0 && (
        <div class="setup-alert setup-alert-todo">
          <p><strong>In no menu at all.</strong> These are live screens that neither navigation list
          carries, so nothing in the product would ever have shown them to you.</p>
          <ul>
            {unlistedVisible.map((u) => (
              <li><a href={u.href}>{u.label}</a> — {u.note}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      {directory.map((g) => (
        <div class="setup-dirgroup">
          <h3 class="setup-dirhead">{g.label}</h3>
          <ul class="setup-dirlist">
            {g.items.map((it) => (
              <li><a href={it.href}>{it.label}</a></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </section>

  </div>

  <style>
    /* 360px first. One column, 44px targets, nothing with a fixed width. */
    .setup-wrap { max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 20px 16px 90px; }
    .setup-head { margin-bottom: 16px; }
    .setup-eyebrow {
      font-size: 10px; font-family: 'Geist Mono', monospace; color: #FF7040;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0 0 5px;
    }
    .setup-title { font-size: 22px; font-weight: 800; color: #fff; margin: 0 0 6px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
    .setup-sub { font-size: 13px; color: #8a8a94; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 4px; }

    .setup-alert { border-radius: 12px; padding: 13px 15px; margin: 0 0 16px; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.65; }
    .setup-alert p { margin: 0; }
    .setup-alert ul { margin: 8px 0 0; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; }
    .setup-alert a { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; font-weight: 700; }
    .setup-alert-bad { background: rgba(239,68,68,0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(239,68,68,0.32); color: #fca5a5; }
    .setup-alert-todo { background: rgba(245,158,11,0.07); border: 1px solid rgba(245,158,11,0.30); color: #fcd34d; }
    .setup-alert-ok { background: rgba(16,185,129,0.07); border: 1px solid rgba(16,185,129,0.26); color: #6ee7b7; }

